WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.630
American Horror Story has covered plenty of
terrifying ground, so there are bound to have

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:07.380
been some huge hits, and some big misses,
along the way.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:11.660
Here are some of the best and worst episodes
of Ryan Murphy's horror anthology juggernaut.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:16.400
American Horror Story broke onto the scene
with Murder House, a traditional haunted house

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.230
story of a dysfunctional family who unwittingly
move into a doomed new home.

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:24.310
The Harmon family are trying to weather plenty
of family issues, including Ben's affair and

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:28.539
Vivien's miscarriage, and encounter a number
of odd neighbors, including the brooding Tate

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:31.940
and the controlling Constance, as they uncover
the dark history of the house.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.320
"Why'd you move here?"

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:34.100
"My dad had an affair…

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.860
My mom literally caught him in the act."

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:38.080
"That's horrible."

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:43.219
In "Piggy, Piggy," the season's sixth episode,
one specific mystery is solved: It turns out

00:00:43.219 --> 00:00:46.730
Tate is not a neighbor at all, but a ghost
who resides in the house.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:50.550
Tate, however, is unaware that he is dead,
and audiences are introduced to Billie Dean

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:52.960
Howard, a medium who can help him with this
discovery.

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:58.339
In classic AHS tradition, the episode is jam-packed,
including a revelation that Vivien is pregnant

00:00:58.339 --> 00:01:02.680
with the Antichrist and a subplot involving
one of Ben's troubled psychiatry patients,

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:06.650
but the emotional core remains with Violet
and Tate, who confess their love for one another

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:09.940
and stand by each other through each frightening
realization that they encounter.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:14.620
Though it wasn't immediately obvious that
Cult would focus on the 2016 election, Murphy

00:01:14.621 --> 00:01:18.240
announced the inspiration before it aired,
setting audiences up for what was sure to

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:19.660
be a polarizing season.

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:24.120
With regulars Sarah Paulson and Evan Peters
taking the helm as a "liberal snowflake" and

00:01:24.121 --> 00:01:28.240
a power-hungry right-winger, respectively,
the season had a strong concept…

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:32.320
Unfortunately, in standard American Horror
Story fashion, it tried a little too hard

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.810
to juggle too much, including more unnerving
neighbors, a succession of influential cult

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:39.730
leaders, a roving band of killer clowns, and
more.

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:43.950
The seventh episode of the seventh season,
"Valerie Solanas Died For Your Sins, Scumbag,"

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:48.320
utilizes a relatively clumsy flashback to
feminist figure Valerie Solanas, who shot

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.900
Andy Warhol in 1968 at the Factory as an act
of rebellion.

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:53.220
"Who are you kidding?

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.820
Shooting me will be the only thing you'll
be remembered for.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.520
I am your legacy."

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:06.480
Though this did actually happen, and Solanas
was a real person, the script takes some fairly

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:10.399
wild liberties with her story, suggesting
that, somehow, she was responsible for all

00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:12.069
of the Zodiac Killer correspondence.

00:02:12.069 --> 00:02:16.940
Yeah, it was kind of a big mess, and all its
muddled story beats made for an unsatisfying

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:17.440
episode.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:22.260
One of the more whimsical seasons of the show,
the third season of American Horror Story

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:27.160
focuses on a coven led by Fiona Goode, played
by Jessica Lange, the ruling Supreme who will

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:29.000
do anything to hold onto her throne.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.920
Meanwhile, the young witches in Fiona's coven
attend a unique Academy, with Goode's daughter

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:34.960
Cordelia acting as headmistress.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:39.100
Throughout the season, each witch hones her
powers, wielding them against outside forces

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.820
while fine-tuning them in the hopes of becoming
the next Supreme.

00:02:42.069 --> 00:02:45.980
The season premiere, "Bitchcraft," gives viewers
a perfect introduction to the season's enormous

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:50.000
coterie of characters, which will only grow
throughout the season, setting up the fight

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.060
for Supreme, as well as introducing us to
a couple of truly terrifying villains.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:58.260
Throughout all of this setup, the premiere
doesn't feel clumsy or expositional, but rather,

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:02.060
like it's prepping viewers for the emotional,
and pulse-pounding, rollercoaster we've been

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:02.640
waiting for.

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:04.780
"Jesus, Sabrina, relax.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:06.480
We're just messing with you."

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.220
Though Coven was a positively received season,
and it began with a proverbial bang, it ended

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:11.840
with a whimper.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:16.060
With the season building to one question,
who will be the next Supreme?, the coven of

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:18.540
witches are all prepared to battle it out
for the title.

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:22.740
As each witch makes her way through the tests,
she's stumped by one element or another.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:26.440
In the end, Cordelia winds up coming through
as the Supreme… which, after pitting the

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:30.430
girls against each other all season, feels
utterly underwhelming, and a little bit like

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:31.120
a cop-out.

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:32.520
"Having fun yet?"

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:34.760
"Yeah, it's a real party."

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:39.540
Even an appearance by Stevie Nicks, who opens
the episode with a short impromptu music video,

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:43.489
can't save this lukewarm finale, which culminates
in Fiona finding herself trapped in her own

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:48.080
personal hell, being stuck in an old farmhouse
for eternity with her former lover, the Axeman.

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:52.000
Watching Jessica Lange frantically scream
about the house's foundations does elicit

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:55.959
some chuckles, but, as a whole, this ending
doesn't do any service to the rollercoaster

00:03:55.959 --> 00:03:57.400
of a season that preceded it.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:02.590
When it comes to American Horror Story, it's
not uncommon for a season to premiere strong…

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:06.489
only to start losing steam as plots become
more and more convoluted and characters come

00:04:06.489 --> 00:04:06.980
and go.

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:11.760
Freak Show is no exception, even though the
season ranks quite low among the others, "Monsters

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.120
Among Us" is a standout episode in its own
right.

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:18.120
Audiences meet Elsa Mars, played by Jessica
Lange in her final full season of American

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:22.320
Horror Story, as she rescues conjoined twins
Bette and Dot from a hospital, only to place

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.320
them in her freak show.

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:27.900
The episode also seamlessly introduces Twisty,
a terrifying, murderous clown, as well as

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.320
socialite Gloria Mott and her deranged son
Dandy.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:31.040
"How's that?"

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:32.340
"Better.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:34.220
The seat's warm."

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:36.160
"Mother made it toasty for you."

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:36.880
Gross.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:41.880
Anyway, to cap it all off, Elsa performs a
David Bowie song, the aptly named "Life on

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:46.330
Mars," to be precise, to close out the episode,
and reveals her own secret: she's a double

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:46.820
amputee.

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:50.900
The premiere of Freak Show is an excellent
example of a successful start: it expertly

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:55.380
sets up the the rest of the season, and despite
the lackluster episodes that follow, it remains

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:57.560
one of the standouts of the entire series.

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:02.800
Even though it had an extraordinarily strong
start, Freak Show, much like its predecessor

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:04.270
Coven, ended with a fizzle.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:08.590
Most of the finale focuses on Elsa herself,
who moves to Hollywood and marries an executive,

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:12.850
but allows herself to be escorted to the afterlife
by the ghost of Edward Mordrake, who kills

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:14.730
any freak that performs on Halloween.

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:15.220
Why?

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:17.720
Because American Horror Story, that's why!

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:22.480
"The day of reckoning is here."

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:26.300
"It hurts… but only for a moment."

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:30.600
In her final performance, she sings David
Bowie's "Heroes," before being gently escorted

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:34.160
back to the freak show, where her deceased
brethren are waiting with open arms.

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:38.480
Though it seems like a fairly warm and happy
ending to such a violent season, it not only

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:42.360
leaves plenty of narrative threads in the
dust, it's also a straight-up dull closing

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:44.560
to an arc that once held so much promise.

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:49.240
Unlike Asylum, which provides closure by giving
Lana Winters one last act of revenge, Freak

00:05:49.241 --> 00:05:53.200
Show pretends it's giving viewers a satisfying
ending, when ultimately it looks more like

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:57.139
the showrunners weren't sure where to go with
such a challenging and off-the-wall season.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:00.020
"The sins of the living don't add up to much
around here.

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:03.420
In life, we play the parts we're cast in."

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:07.940
It's consistently ranked as the best season
of the entire series, so it's difficult to

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:09.640
choose the best episode of Asylum.

00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:13.500
The finale, in which Lana Winters takes her
revenge on her murderous son and triumphs

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:17.980
by force of sheer will, was a strong contender,
but the top spot has to go to "Name Game,"

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:21.800
if only for the trippy, out-of-place dance
sequence starring Jessica Lange from which

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:23.060
the episode takes its title.

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:26.080
Yeah, it's a wacky one in all the right ways.

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:29.800
This episode is a perfect showcase for the
best of everything that American Horror Story

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:35.080
has to offer; sheer fantasy, indelible images,
and dramatic moments; including a touching

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:39.150
turn by Frances Conroy as the Angel of Death,
who arrives to collect the dead throughout

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:39.780
the episode.

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:43.860
When it's at the top of its game, the show
looks a lot like this: a love letter to both

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:48.150
camp and horror, while still letting each
character experience real, human moments that

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:50.880
remind us of why we fell in love with it in
the first place.

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:52.920
"Wanted to say goodbye."

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:54.540
"Goodbye?"

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:56.720
"I'm going to Rome."

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:02.400
If Asylum is frequently ranked as the best
season of American Horror Story, then Hotel,

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:05.540
which usually falls to the bottom of the pack,
is its direct inverse.

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:10.720
The season is based loosely on several hotel-related
horror stories, including H.H. Holmes' murderous

00:07:10.729 --> 00:07:15.639
hotel, where the serial killer kept his victims
during the 1893 World's Fair in Chicago, and

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:18.980
the 2013 disappearance of Elisa Lam at L.A.'s
Cecil Hotel.

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:22.790
With Jessica Lange's exit at the end of the
previous season, the show had yet another

00:07:22.790 --> 00:07:27.050
hurdle to overcome, who would serve as the
leading lady, and though Lady Gaga offered

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:31.470
up an admirably campy turn as the vampiric
Countess, it couldn't save an uneven season

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:35.260
that tried a little too hard and muddled its
own story time and time again.

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:39.360
After all, would it even be American Horror
Story if it didn't have too many arcs and

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:41.540
way, way too many unlikable characters?

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:43.800
"Who the hell are you people?"

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:48.660
The premiere remains the worst episode of
what many viewers consider one of the worst

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:52.140
seasons, if only for its best known scene:
a violent sequence during which Gabriel, played

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:56.289
by New Girl's Max Greenfield, is sodomized
by a monster known as the "Addiction Demon."

00:07:56.289 --> 00:08:00.770
Critics and fans alike viewed this as exploitative
and unnecessary, and it became clear that

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:04.910
while American Horror Story was usually a
guilty pleasure, this season might be abandoning

00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:06.400
the word "pleasure" entirely.

